    {
      "id": "0001",
      "front": "Describe how to solve a separable first-order differential equation.",
      "back": "Rearrange so that every y term sits with dy and every x term with dx, integrate both sides, and add one arbitrary constant to give the general solution.",
      "answerType": "SELF_GRADE",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "separate the variables",
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "integrate both sides",
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "one arbitrary constant",
          "synonyms": [
            "constant of integration"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Only one constant is needed even though two integrals are taken, because they differ by a constant that can be absorbed.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Core Pure 2: Differential equations",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
        "further-maths",
        "core-pure-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0002",
      "front": "Solve $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x$ given that $y = 1$ when $x = 0$.",
      "back": "$y = x^2 + 1$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "y = x^2 + 1",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$y = x^2 + 1$",
        "distractors": [
          "$y = x^2$",
          "$y = 2x^2 + 1$",
          "$y = x + 1$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Integrating gives $y = x^2 + c$, and the initial condition forces $c = 1$.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Core Pure 2: Differential equations",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
        "further-maths",
        "core-pure-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0003",
      "front": "What is the integrating factor for $\\frac{dy}{dx} + P(x)y = Q(x)$?",
      "back": "$e^{\\int P(x)\\,dx}$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "e to the integral of P dx",
          "synonyms": [
            "exp(\u222bP dx)"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "acceptedAliases": [
        "IF = e^\u222bP dx"
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$e^{\\int P(x)\\,dx}$",
        "distractors": [
          "$e^{-\\int P(x)\\,dx}$",
          "$\\int P(x)\\,dx$",
          "$P(x)e^{x}$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Multiplying through by it turns the left-hand side into the exact derivative of the product of the integrating factor and y, so no constant of integration is needed in the exponent.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Core Pure 2: Differential equations",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
        "further-maths",
        "core-pure-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0004",
      "front": "State the general solution of $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} + 4y = 0$.",
      "back": "$y = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "A cos 2x + B sin 2x",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$y = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x$",
        "distractors": [
          "$y = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-2x}$",
          "$y = A\\cos 4x + B\\sin 4x$",
          "$y = (A + Bx)e^{2x}$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "The auxiliary equation $m^2 + 4 = 0$ gives $m = \\pm 2i$, so the solution oscillates with angular frequency 2 rather than growing.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Core Pure 2: Differential equations",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
        "further-maths",
        "core-pure-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0005",
      "front": "When the auxiliary equation of a second-order equation has a repeated root $m$, what form does the complementary function take?",
      "back": "$y = (A + Bx)e^{mx}$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "(A + Bx)e^(mx)",
          "synonyms": [
            "A + Bx times e to the mx"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "acceptedAliases": [
        "y = (A + Bx)exp(mx)"
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$y = (A + Bx)e^{mx}$",
        "distractors": [
          "$y = Ae^{mx} + Be^{-mx}$",
          "$y = (A + Bx^2)e^{mx}$",
          "$y = Ae^{mx}$ only"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "A single exponential gives only one arbitrary constant, so it is multiplied by x to produce the second independent solution the two integrations require.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Core Pure 2: Differential equations",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
